<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zambezi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zambezi is the fourth-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, flowing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, along the border of Zambia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. It was a major route for trade between the interior and the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> arose on the plateau south of the Zambezi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a> channelled gold exports through trading fairs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zumbo">Zumbo</a> on the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zambezi.jpg" alt="Zambezi" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian McMorrow · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zambezi_River_at_junction_of_Namibia,_Zambia,_Zimbabwe_%26_Botswana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The river is 2,574 km long and drains a basin of 1,390,000 km2, slightly less than half the size of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>'s. It rises in a marshy dambo in miombo woodland in North-Western Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, and flows south-west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> for about 240 km before turning south. It then runs along the north-eastern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and the northern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, follows the border between Zambia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, and crosses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p><p>The upper river falls about 400 m between its source and the Chavuma Falls, then only another 180 m across some 800 km to Victoria Falls, which mark the boundary with the middle river. Below the falls the river runs for about 200 km through the Batoka Gorge, between basalt walls 20 to 60 m apart. The lower river, 650 km from Cahora Bassa to the sea, is navigable but shallow in the dry season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> anchored in January 1498 at what he called the Rio dos Bons Sinais, now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quelimane">Quelimane</a>, which was then linked to the Zambezi by navigable channels. By the 16th century the river was called the Cuama, after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast outpost in its delta. Medieval geographers knew the region as the Empire of Monomotapa, and early maps probably drew on Arab information. The Portuguese degredado António Fernandes visited the inland river in 1511 and 1513 to report on trade; his findings showed the importance of the upper Zambezi ports to the East African gold trade.</p><p>The rapids prevented the river from becoming a long-distance route. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a>'s Zambezi expedition tried to open it to paddle steamers but was defeated by the Cahora Bassa rapids. A paddle-barge service later ran between the Katombora Rapids and the rapids above Katima Mulilo, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lozi-people">Lozi</a> paddlers and teams of oxen hauling barges round the Ngonye Falls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lake Kariba, created in 1959 behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kariba-dam">Kariba Dam</a>, is one of the largest man-made lakes in the world, and the Cahora Bassa Dam in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> was completed in 1973. Together the dams control the seasonal floods, and the delta is now about half as broad as before. Filling Cahora Bassa in a single flood season cut mangrove cover by 40 per cent and the prawn catch by 60 per cent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zambezi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Barotseland grew out of the Bulozi floodplain of the upper <b>Zambezi</b> in what is now western Zambia, a broad grassland flooded each year between about January and June.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Zimbabwe grew up on the granite country of the Zimbabwe plateau, between the Limpopo and <b>Zambezi</b> rivers, where Bantu-speaking farmers had lived since the first millennium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mutapa Empire, also known as the state of the Mwene Mutapa, arose in the mid-fifteenth century in the region between the <b>Zambezi</b> and Limpopo rivers in present-day Zimbabwe and Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rhodes, whose main interest lay south of the <b>Zambezi</b>, wanted the north held as cheaply as possible and sent emissaries to make treaties with chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He drove them back towards the <b>Zambezi</b> valley and the Mozambican lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Korekore tradition says Nyatsimba Mutota of the royal family led people north and founded the Mutapa Empire, and a new trade route along the <b>Zambezi</b> favoured Khami.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
